\section{Selecting rows}{

Give either \code{rows} or \code{rownames}, one selection per model table.
\itemize{
\item \code{rows}: body row numbers of each model table (row 1 is the first row
below the two header rows, counting factor headings and reference
rows): a list of integer vectors, \code{list(1, 3:5)}, or Stata's string
\code{"1 \\\\ 3/5"}. With a single model table a plain vector will do.
\item \code{rownames}: case-insensitive patterns matched against the displayed
row labels, as substrings (\code{"dose"} matches \code{"  Low dose"}, and
\code{"male"} matches \code{"  Female"}), with
Stata's wildcards \code{*} (any text) and \verb{?} (one character): a list of
character vectors, one per model table, each element one pattern
(\code{list("hrt", c("low", "medium", "high"))}), or Stata's string
\code{"hrt \\\\ low medium high"}. As in Stata, a table's patterns given as
one string are split at blanks (\code{"low high"} is two patterns; quote a
phrase, \code{'"2 or more"'}), while a vector of several strings is taken
element by element (\code{c("low", "2 or more")}). Every pattern must match
at least one row, and a pattern may match several; a blank pattern is
refused (in Stata it selects every row). With \code{rownames_exact = TRUE}
a pattern must match a whole label instead (ignoring case and the
label's indent; \code{*} and \verb{?} still apply), so \code{"treated"} selects
\code{Treated} but not \code{Untreated}.
}

In vertical mode the rows of each table are shown in the table's own
order, each once, whatever the order of the selection. In rate mode the
selected rows are consumed exposure block by exposure block, in the rate
table's order: a block with \code{k} categories takes the next \code{k - 1} rows,
so the total must be the number of categories minus one per block.
Within a block each row goes to the category with the same label
(ignoring case and surrounding blanks), never by position, so the order
inside a block does not matter; the category left over is the reference,
and for a factor level it must be that model's own base level (a model
fitted with another base level shows its reference on that category).
The one positional case is a plain row, such as a 0/1 indicator, whose
label matches no category of a two-category block: it fills the second
category, so code the indicator 1 for that category. Everything that
cannot be placed exactly is an error: a heading or reference row, a
level that matches no category, two rows for one category, or a
reference category that is not the model's base level.
}

\section{Matching rates to models}{

Each model table must hold one model per outcome of the rate table, and
they are matched by identity, never by position: the rate table's
\code{outcomeids} against each model's outcome identity (for a
\code{survival::coxph()} fit, the event variable of \code{Surv()}), both
lower-cased and trimmed. \code{outcomemap} names, in the rate table's outcome
order, the model to use for each outcome instead: a model identity,
outcome identity or model label (as \verb{models =} gave it) that must match
exactly one model in every model table. The models must be on the
hazard-ratio scale (the estimate header \code{HR}, \code{aHR}, \verb{Hazard ratio} or
\verb{Adjusted hazard ratio}, as \verb{coef =} sets it) and at the rate table's
confidence level; rate tables with \code{rateratio = TRUE} are refused, as in
Stata.

In vertical mode the model columns of every table are aligned to the
first table's: by outcome identity when those are unique and non-blank,
else by model label, else by model identity; every table must hold the
same models, at the same confidence level and with the same statistics.
}

\section{Differences from Stata}{

\itemize{
\item R has no frames: the model and rate tables are \code{tt_table} objects (or
\code{\link[=as.data.frame]{as.data.frame()}} of them), and the result stands for \code{frame()}. The
stored \code{rateframe} and \code{modelframes} hold the arguments as written.
\item Like the other commands, R prints no \verb{Exported ...} or \verb{Markdown exported to ...} lines.
\item Rate mode refuses an \code{\link[=effecttab]{effecttab()}} model of differences, predictions
or raw log ratios even when its \code{effect} header says HR. A log-ratio
comparison must record an explicit \code{transform = exp} (or \code{"exp"})
to establish its ratio scale; an unevaluated transform symbol or custom
function cannot establish it. Alternatively supply a data frame of
exponentiated estimates and intervals. Rate mode also refuses an
indicator row (not a factor level) for a block whose two categories
read Yes/No, 1/0 or TRUE/FALSE, where Stata's rule (the indicator is
the second category) would show it under the "No" category.
\item The stored \code{statistic_ids} of a vertical table merged with \code{compact}
is \code{"estimate_ci pvalue"}; Stata keeps the sources' \code{"estimate ci pvalue"}.
\item R's model identities differ from Stata's command lines, and a
\code{survival::coxph()} fit's outcome identity is its event variable
(Stata's \code{stcox} models all have \verb{_t}), so rate tables and Cox models
match without \code{outcomemap} when \code{outcomeids} name the event variables.
A model table without model identities (an \code{\link[=effecttab]{effecttab()}} of a data
frame) is refused only when the models have to be aligned by identity.
\item \code{forest} (drawing the plot) is not ported; \code{\link[=as_forest_data]{as_forest_data()}} gives
the data. For a vertical table its \code{model}, \code{model_label} and \code{label}
are the composite's, as the table shows them: a model table whose
models were aligned in another order keeps each model in its
composite column, and \code{relabel} text replaces the row label. Stata's
\code{eplotframe()} copies the source table's model index and labels; R
keeps that index in \code{source_model}.
\item \code{rows}, \code{rownames}, \code{section}, \code{outcomemap} and \code{relabel} take R
vectors and lists as well as Stata's strings.
\item \code{rownames_exact} is R only: Stata's \code{rownames()} always matches
substrings.
}
}

\section{Session destinations}{

An explicitly supplied non-NULL \code{sheet} enables inherited workbook and
Markdown destinations from \code{\link[=tabtools_options]{tabtools_options()}}. Default or NULL sheet does
not request inherited output. Explicit paths still export, and explicit NULL
destinations opt out. A requested sheet without a workbook gives
\code{tabtools_warning_sheet_without_workbook} before output; \code{options(warn = 2)}
interrupts the call. See \code{\link[=tabtools_options]{tabtools_options()}} for append/history behavior.
}

\examples{
\dontshow{if (requireNamespace("survival", quietly = TRUE)) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
d <- survival::lung
d$event <- d$status - 1
d$years <- d$time / 365.25
d$sex <- factor(d$sex, 1:2, c("Male", "Female"))
d$ecog <- factor(pmin(d$ph.ecog, 2), 0:2, c("0", "1", "2+"))
d <- d[!is.na(d$ecog), ]

# Events, person-years and rates per 100 person-years by sex
rates <- stratetab(tt_rates(d, "years", "event", by = "sex"),
                   outlabels = "Death", outcomeids = "event",
                   explabels = "Sex", ratescale = 100, unitlabel = "100")

# Crude and adjusted hazard ratios, one model per table
crude <- regtab(survival::coxph(survival::Surv(years, event) ~ sex, data = d,
                                ties = "breslow"), coef = "HR")
adj <- regtab(survival::coxph(survival::Surv(years, event) ~ sex + age + ecog, data = d,
                              ties = "breslow"), coef = "HR")

# Table 2: the rate table with the adjusted HR of Female vs Male
hrcomptab(rates, adj, rownames = "female")

# Vertical composite: the sex rows of both models, stacked
comptab(list(crude, adj), rownames = list("female", "female"),
        section = c("Crude", "Adjusted for age and ECOG"), compact = TRUE)
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
}
\seealso{
\code{\link[=stratetab]{stratetab()}} and \code{\link[=tt_rates]{tt_rates()}} for the rates; \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}} and
\code{\link[=effecttab]{effecttab()}} for the models; \code{\link[=stacktab]{stacktab()}} to stack whole sheets.
}
